<h2>A staples-driven market</h2>
<p>Iraq's Indian import profile is concentrated and consistent: basmati rice as a dietary staple, banana as a major fresh-fruit line, plus lentils and cardamom. It's one of the clearest examples of India supplying core food-security commodities rather than niche or premium goods.</p>

<h2>Rice and bananas ship differently</h2>
<p>Basmati is a dry-cargo product with no cold-chain requirement and a long shelf life, well suited to the 8–14 day transit to Umm Qasr. Banana is perishable and needs an unbroken reefer chain at 13–14°C for the same transit — still within the fruit's 21–28 day shelf life, but with no margin for cold-chain failure. If you're sourcing both, expect very different logistics conversations for each.</p>

<h2>Documentation takes longer here than in the Gulf</h2>
<p>Certificate of origin attestation for Iraq typically involves chamber of commerce sign-off and sometimes consular legalisation — steps that add real processing days compared with more streamlined Gulf markets. Build this into your shipping timeline from the start rather than discovering it after the vessel books.</p>

